## 2. The unbounded diagonal produces one fixed enlargement

Put \(\alpha_D^t(A)=e^{itKD}Ae^{-itKD}\). For an operator supported in \(X\), all diagonal edge terms disjoint from X commute with A and with every remaining edge term. They therefore cancel from this conjugation exactly. Consequently

\[
\alpha_D^t(A_X)\in\mathcal A_{X^+},\qquad
X^+=X\cup\bigcup_{x\in X}N(x),\qquad
\|\alpha_D^t(A_X)\|=\|A_X\|.
\tag{7}
\]

There is no iteration to a second neighborhood: that would count diagonal terms that already cancelled. The statement holds uniformly in t and K. Conjugation is strongly and strong-adjoint continuous, by the strongly continuous diagonal unitary group. Operator-norm continuity of (7) is neither assumed nor generally true.

In the interaction picture, \(V_{ac}(s)=\alpha_D^s(V_{ac})\) is a bounded, self-adjoint, strongly continuous interaction on \(Y_{ac}^+\), with unchanged norm. Sufficient uniform parameters are

\[
\max|Y_{ac}^+|\le M:=98,\qquad
\max\operatorname{diam}(Y_{ac}^+)\le R:=6,\qquad
\sup_x\sum_{ac:Y_{ac}^+\ni x}\sup_s\|V_{ac}(s)\|
\le\nu:=(1+z)\nu_{\rm orig}.
\tag{8}
\]

The final inequality follows by charging any enlarged support containing x to an original support containing some vertex in \(\{x\}\cup N(x)\). These are operator-norm bounds on the whole ambient P space, independent of electric values. The same one-neighborhood argument applies to the jumps in this picture, although the comparison below needs only Hamiltonian locality.

For each finite volume, \(h=KD+V_\Lambda\) is self-adjoint on \(\mathcal D(D)\). Its interaction-picture propagator is the vector Dyson series for the finite sum of the bounded strongly continuous \(V_{ac}(s)\). The norm majorant of its nth term is \((t\sum\|V_{ac}\|)^n/n!\); it converges on every vector. The unbounded free unitary then restores the Schrödinger propagator. On trace class this gives a strongly continuous unitary channel. The finite sum of bounded formation maps is a bounded generator perturbation. Equivalently its interaction-picture approximation by completely positive finite-step evolutions gives the trace-preserving completely positive semigroup supplied by the common-limit source.

For mathematical cross-checking, [Nachtergaele, Sims and Young, arXiv:1810.02428v1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1810.02428v1), Proposition 2.2, Proposition 2.4 and Lemma 3.2, cover bounded strongly continuous time-dependent interactions on possibly infinite-dimensional site spaces. Their complete needed proofs and Section 3.1 were read. The commuting **edge** diagonal here first requires (7); it cannot be imported as an onsite Hamiltonian. The following chain and cubic-volume estimates are the specialized reconstruction used here. No infinite-volume theorem from that paper is imported.

## 3. A sufficient Hamiltonian commutator bound

Let \(\tau_h^t(A)=e^{ith}Ae^{-ith}\). With \(U_I\) the bounded interaction-picture propagator,

\[
\tau_h^t(O_X)=U_I(t,0)^*\alpha_D^t(O_X)U_I(t,0).
\tag{9}
\]

For each fixed endpoint t, the middle operator is simply a bounded input in \(X^+\). Its dependence on the endpoint need not be norm differentiable to apply a locality bound for \(U_I\).

Here is the needed commutator iteration. For an interaction-picture evolution \(\tau_I\), define
\(C_B(Y,u)=\sup_{A\in\mathcal A_Y,\|A\|\le1}\|[\tau_I^{u,0}(A),B]\|\).
Removing the evolution internal to Y before differentiating gives

\[
C_B(Y,u)\le2\|B\|1_{Y\cap\operatorname{supp}B\ne\varnothing}
+2\sum_{Z:Z\cap Y\ne\varnothing}\int_0^u
\|V_Z(s)\|C_B(Z,s)\,ds.
\tag{10}
\]

More precisely, only interactions crossing the boundary of Y occur; allowing all intersecting Z only increases the right side. To justify the removal without a norm derivative, differentiate the product of the two bounded propagators strongly. The Jacobi identity puts one contribution in the form \(i[C(s),f(s)]\) with bounded self-adjoint C. Conjugating by its unitary propagator leaves an inhomogeneous strong integral, whose norm is at most the integral of the source norms. This proves (10) and uses no commutator with D.

Iterating (10) gives chains \(Z_1,\ldots,Z_n\), each touching the preceding support, with \(Z_1\cap X^+\ne\varnothing\) and \(Z_n\cap\operatorname{supp}B\ne\varnothing\). Write \(s_X=|X^+|\). Dropping the final condition, the sum of products of the norms for chains of length \(n\ge1\) is bounded by

\[
s_X\nu(M\nu)^{n-1}=\frac{s_X}{M}(M\nu)^n.
\tag{11}
\]

The first sum costs \(s_X\nu\), and every following intersection costs at most \(M\nu\). Together with the simplex factor \((2u)^n/n!\), this also makes the uniterated remainder tend to zero. For \(d=d(X^+,\operatorname{supp}B)\), a chain can terminate only if \(nR\ge d\). Inserting \(1_{nR\ge d}\le e^{nR-d}\) and also using the trivial commutator norm gives

\[
\|[\tau_h^u(O_X),B]\|
\le2\|O_X\|\|B\|
\min\{1,s_Xe^{a_Hu-d(X^+,\operatorname{supp}B)}\},
\qquad a_H:=2M\nu e^R.
\tag{12}
\]

For overlapping supports the length-zero term is at most one; the same formula holds since \(s_X\ge1\). The factors in (8) show \(a_H=A_H\delta\) with a degree-dependent constant \(A_H\). It is a sufficient bound, not an actual signal velocity. These estimates do not require spatially uncorrelated states or finite-dimensional local Hilbert spaces.

## 4. Sum the formation source locally, then use Duhamel

For each bounded B, the Heisenberg dissipator satisfies the exact identity

\[
\mathcal D[B]^*(A)
=\tfrac12\bigl(B^*[A,B]+[B^*,A]B\bigr).
\tag{13}
\]

Apply (12) to both B and \(B^*\). Marks centered at a have support within the radius-one star, so \(d(X^+,\operatorname{supp}B_j)\ge d(X^+,a)-1\). Therefore

\[
\sum_j\|\mathcal D[B_j]^*\tau_h^u(O_X)\|
\le2\|O_X\|b_c\sum_{a\in A}
\min\{1,s_Xe^{a_Hu+1-d(X^+,a)}\}.
\tag{14}
\]

The torus graph ball count is bounded uniformly by the integer-lattice count: choose a unique minimal coordinate lift for each torus point. For radius \(r\ge1\), the number of points at distance r in \(\mathbb Z^3\) is \(4r^2+2\). This identity follows by separating vectors with one, two and three nonzero coordinates; their counts are \(6\), \(12(r-1)\) and \(4(r-1)(r-2)\). The zero-radius count is one.

For \(s\ge0\), set \(R_0=\lceil s\rceil\). The sum of \(\min(1,e^{s-d(x,a)})\) inside radius \(R_0\) is at most \(8(R_0+1)^3\). Outside, with \(n=r-R_0\ge1\), it is at most

\[
6(R_0+1)^2\sum_{n\ge1}(n+1)^2e^{-n}
<30(R_0+1)^2.
\]

For example \(e^{-1}<3/8\), and the last power series at \(3/8\) is exactly \(579/125<5\). Thus, in every finite torus,

\[
\sum_a\min(1,e^{s-d(x,a)})\le40(s+2)^3.
\tag{15}
\]

Use \(e^{-d(X^+,a)}\le\sum_{x\in X^+}e^{-d(x,a)}\) and \(\min(1,\sum_xr_x)\le\sum_x\min(1,r_x)\). Equations (14)-(15) with \(s=a_Hu+1+\log s_X\) give the explicit polynomial source estimate

\[
\sum_j\|\mathcal D[B_j]^*\tau_h^u(O_X)\|
\le80b_c\|O_X\|s_X
\bigl(3+\log s_X+a_Hu\bigr)^3.
\tag{16}
\]

For comparison, directly summing the terminal marks in the chain iteration with the per-site budget \(b_s\) gives the sometimes better short-time estimate

\[
\sum_j\|\mathcal D[B_j]^*\tau_h^u(O_X)\|
\le2\|O_X\|s_Xb_s e^{2M\nu u}.
\tag{17}
\]

Let \(T_\kappa(t)=e^{t\mathcal L_\kappa^*}\) and \(T_0(t)=\tau_h^t\). Finite-volume bounded-perturbation Duhamel, first on trace class and then dualized, is

\[
T_\kappa(t)O-T_0(t)O
=\kappa\int_0^tT_\kappa(s)
\left(\sum_j\mathcal D[B_j]^*T_0(t-s)O\right)ds.
\tag{18}
\]

The integral is interpreted ultraweakly, or by pairing with an arbitrary trace-class input. \(T_\kappa\) is a unital completely positive contraction. Pairing (18) with trace-class \(\rho\), using \(\|e^{s\mathcal L_\kappa}\rho\|_1\le\|\rho\|_1\), and inserting (16), proves

\[
\|T_\kappa(t)O_X-T_0(t)O_X\|
\le \min\left\{2\|O_X\|,
80\kappa b_c\|O_X\|s_X
\int_0^t(3+\log s_X+a_Hu)^3du\right\}.
\tag{19}
\]

One may also take the minimum with the integral of (17). Since \(s_X\le7|X|\) and \(a_H=A_H\delta\), (19) implies (1). It is uniform over \(0\le t\le T\) by replacing t by T on the right. It compares the full open evolution with the full Hamiltonian evolution, not with an evolution in a single charge sector.

Although the total dissipator norm in a finite box grows with volume, that norm was used only to justify its finite-volume existence and Duhamel identity. Its size does not enter (19). Only the local commutator sum enters the final bound. No electric moment, \([D,O_X]\) bound, differentiability of O, or initial domain membership is required for (18)-(19). This cancellation of the common unbounded Hamiltonian is the reason such a uniform comparison is possible.

## 5. The actual full-ensemble factorial statistic

Let \(\rho_\kappa(t)=e^{t\mathcal L_\kappa}\rho\). For two specified original marks i,j, the ordered factorial moment density at times t and \(t+u\), \(u>0\), is

\[
G^{(2)}_{ij,\kappa}(t,u)=\kappa^2\operatorname{Tr}\left[
B_j^*B_j\ e^{u\mathcal L_\kappa}
 (B_i\rho_\kappa(t)B_i^*)\right].
\tag{20}
\]

To derive it, insert the two selected jump CP maps in the trajectory expansion and sum all other marks, at all other times, before and between the insertions. Those sums give the full trace-preserving semigroups in (20). Other events are unrestricted before and between the insertions; later events sum out by trace preservation. The change from ordered times \((t,t+u)\) to \((t,u)\) has unit Jacobian. Finite volume and bounded channels justify the trace-class Dyson expansion. Here number raising actually bounds the number of total formations by the available records, while h and loss preserve record number.

Strong trace-class continuity of the full semigroup and boundedness of the two maps give

\[
G^{(2)}_{ij,\kappa}(t,0+)=\kappa^2\operatorname{Tr}[O_{ij}\rho_\kappa(t)],
\qquad O_{ij}=B_i^*B_j^*B_jB_i\ge0.
\tag{21}
\]

It is a bounded local observable on **all** P matter sectors, supported in the union of the two A stars. The positive-age propagator in (20) is the complete \(KD+\delta H_4^C\) Hamiltonian with the complete original dissipator on all record sectors. Neither a no-event propagator nor the initial field-only Hamiltonian replaces it.

Equation (21) is a one-sided boundary density. It is not a point mass for two simultaneous marks: the finite-volume bounded-rate process assigns zero probability to two marks at exactly the same time.

Use the previously checked geometry
